WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.305
The Model-based Embedded and
Robotic Systems Group at MIT

00:00:03.305 --> 00:00:05.430
is collaborating with the
Jet Propulsion Laboratory

00:00:05.430 --> 00:00:08.220
at Caltech on a project
that will enable astronauts

00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:11.950
to safely work with robots
in an easy and intuitive way.

00:00:11.950 --> 00:00:14.190
One example of such
a robot is ATHLETE,

00:00:14.190 --> 00:00:15.870
a vehicle developed
at JPL that will

00:00:15.870 --> 00:00:17.670
support robotic
and human missions

00:00:17.670 --> 00:00:19.350
on the surface of the Moon.

00:00:19.350 --> 00:00:21.330
ATHLETE is capable
of rolling or walking

00:00:21.330 --> 00:00:23.190
over extremely rough
or steep terrain,

00:00:23.190 --> 00:00:25.890
and can load, transport,
manipulate, and deposit

00:00:25.890 --> 00:00:28.860
payloads to any desired
sites of interest.

00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:30.780
Previously, robots
have been simple enough

00:00:30.780 --> 00:00:32.580
to be controlled
through teleoperation

00:00:32.580 --> 00:00:34.110
or direct commanding.

00:00:34.110 --> 00:00:37.590
However, more complex robots,
like ATHLETE or the Vecna BEAR

00:00:37.590 --> 00:00:39.810
battlefield robot,
have too many degrees

00:00:39.810 --> 00:00:42.240
of freedom for direct
teleoperation or commanding

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:43.620
to be practical.

00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:45.540
We've developed a
different control paradigm

00:00:45.540 --> 00:00:47.880
that incorporates
verbal commands, shared

00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:49.800
written instructions,
and demonstration

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:52.800
by example to enable more
efficient interaction.

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:55.290
These modes of communication
occur naturally to humans

00:00:55.290 --> 00:00:57.120
during collaborative tasks.

00:00:57.120 --> 00:00:59.700
Here is an example of a
common-sense written plan where

00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:01.290
the human and
athlete work together

00:01:01.290 --> 00:01:03.850
to collect rock
samples for analysis.

00:01:03.850 --> 00:01:05.430
The instructions
include descriptions

00:01:05.430 --> 00:01:08.850
of both the human's tasks
and ATHLETE's tasks.

00:01:08.850 --> 00:01:10.770
A key challenge
to executing tasks

00:01:10.770 --> 00:01:13.980
is grounding terms like pick
up into ATHLETE motions.

00:01:13.980 --> 00:01:15.600
We reduce time and
effort required

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:18.270
to do this by teaching
actions through demonstration.

00:01:18.270 --> 00:01:19.650
We use an interface
device called

00:01:19.650 --> 00:01:23.070
TRACK developed by the
Distributed Robotics Lab at MIT

00:01:23.070 --> 00:01:25.710
to teleoperate the robot
through each desired activity

00:01:25.710 --> 00:01:26.940
several times.

00:01:26.940 --> 00:01:29.042
Here we're teaching
athlete retirement pick up

00:01:29.042 --> 00:01:31.500
by showing athlete how to pick
up an object without hitting

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:32.610
the box.

00:01:32.610 --> 00:01:34.470
Based on the
demonstrated motions,

00:01:34.470 --> 00:01:37.290
are learning algorithm
generalizes from the examples

00:01:37.290 --> 00:01:38.880
a set of motions
that will likely

00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:40.860
achieve the action of pick up.

00:01:40.860 --> 00:01:43.320
This is compactly encoded
in the representation we

00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:46.110
call a probabilistic flow tube.

00:01:46.110 --> 00:01:47.730
Once ATHLETE learns
the activity,

00:01:47.730 --> 00:01:50.250
it can autonomously execute
the corresponding nominal

00:01:50.250 --> 00:01:51.330
trajectory.

00:01:51.330 --> 00:01:53.580
The learned flow tube
represents flexibility

00:01:53.580 --> 00:01:55.710
in the motions the robot
can choose to perform,

00:01:55.710 --> 00:01:58.950
enabling it, for example, to
recover from disturbances.

00:01:58.950 --> 00:02:00.840
The width of the flow
tube indicates areas

00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:03.030
with more or less flexibility.

00:02:03.030 --> 00:02:05.550
After ATHLETE knows how to
perform each of its tasks

00:02:05.550 --> 00:02:08.160
individually, it can
collaborate with the human

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:10.590
to execute the
written plan together.

00:02:10.590 --> 00:02:12.240
We have developed
an executive that

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:14.460
allows ATHLETE to make
decisions at runtime

00:02:14.460 --> 00:02:16.950
to improve the likelihood
of successfully completing

00:02:16.950 --> 00:02:18.150
the plan.

00:02:18.150 --> 00:02:21.210
Specifically, ATHLETE schedules
the start times and durations

00:02:21.210 --> 00:02:23.370
of each activity
with flexibility,

00:02:23.370 --> 00:02:26.580
while ensuring that limbs
synchronize successfully.

00:02:26.580 --> 00:02:29.730
This allows it to react
appropriately to the human

00:02:29.730 --> 00:02:32.190
and still perform the
actions correctly.

00:02:32.190 --> 00:02:34.620
We demonstrated our
capabilities by integrating them

00:02:34.620 --> 00:02:37.830
with the ATHLETE prototype at
JPL, and by showing a human

00:02:37.830 --> 00:02:40.770
and ATHLETE cooperating
to complete this task.

00:02:40.770 --> 00:02:44.380
First, the human attaches a
gripper to ATHLETE's left limb.

00:02:44.380 --> 00:02:47.490
The executive timestamps
this activity duration

00:02:47.490 --> 00:02:50.070
when the person is done so
it can accommodate changes

00:02:50.070 --> 00:02:51.930
in the human's activities.

00:02:51.930 --> 00:02:54.180
Now ATHLETE is moving
the gripper near the rock

00:02:54.180 --> 00:02:55.890
in preparation to pick it up.

00:02:55.890 --> 00:02:59.250
This complex motion was learned
using the techniques described

00:02:59.250 --> 00:03:00.330
earlier.

00:03:00.330 --> 00:03:03.450
Since ATHLETE can't sense the
precise location of the rock,

00:03:03.450 --> 00:03:06.570
the person can assist ATHLETE
by fine-tuning the gripper

00:03:06.570 --> 00:03:08.690
location through
voice commanding.

00:03:08.690 --> 00:03:11.270
Move limb six forward
5 centimeters.

00:03:16.040 --> 00:03:20.120
Next, the human attaches have
been to ATHLETE's right limb.

00:03:20.120 --> 00:03:24.060
The gripper closes on
the rock to pick it up.

00:03:24.060 --> 00:03:26.400
At this point, both
limbs move in position

00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:28.060
to drop the rock into the box.

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:38.600
Finally, ATHLETE stores the
box for transport, which

00:03:38.600 --> 00:03:41.540
is the end goal of the plan.

00:03:41.540 --> 00:03:43.910
In the future, we plan to
demonstrate its capabilities

00:03:43.910 --> 00:03:45.980
with more complex
plans that involve

00:03:45.980 --> 00:03:49.310
the humans safely coordinating
with multiple robots.

00:03:49.310 --> 00:03:52.370
Engineers at JPL are currently
developing the next generation

00:03:52.370 --> 00:03:54.920
of ATHLETE robots
called Tri-ATHLETE,

00:03:54.920 --> 00:03:56.750
in which each robot
has three limbs

00:03:56.750 --> 00:03:59.720
and can coordinate together to
transport habitats and perform

00:03:59.720 --> 00:04:02.330
more complex manipulation tasks.

00:04:02.330 --> 00:04:04.670
In the future, we aim to
show humans working safely

00:04:04.670 --> 00:04:07.040
in the same environment
as the Tri-ATHLETEs

00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:09.830
in order to support future
operation on the Moon.

00:04:09.830 --> 00:04:11.360
Funding for this
task was provided

00:04:11.360 --> 00:04:14.000
through the JPL Strategic
University Research Partnership

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:16.459
Program and the National
Defense Science and Engineering

00:04:16.459 --> 00:04:19.000
Graduate Fellowship.

